  "Number": 24,
  "Question": "When simplified, \\(\\dfrac{2^{n+4}-2(2^n)}{2(2^{n+3})}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2^{n+1}-\\dfrac18\\)",
    "B": "\\(-2^{n+1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1-2^n\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac78\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac74\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The exponent-sum rule lets us write every term using the common factor \\(2^n\\): \\(2^{n+4}=16\\cdot2^n\\) and \\(2^{n+3}=8\\cdot2^n\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{2^{n+4}-2(2^n)}{2(2^{n+3})}\n=\\frac{(16-2)2^n}{16\\cdot2^n}\n=\\frac{14}{16}\n=\\frac78.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(2^n>0\\) for every real \\(n\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac78}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
